ore people to the club. It wakes up the bouncer. - The bouncer (prefrontal cortex) gets a little jolt of dopamine and norepinephrine. - He finally has enough energy to stand up and do his job: "Alright, background noise—out. Visual clutter—out. Tag on the shirt—handled." - The overcrowded club quiets down from 300 people to the manageable 100 it was designed for. The feeling of calm isn't sedation. It's the profound relief of the chaos *finally* stopping. The person wasn't craving more noise; they were drowning in unfiltered noise and desperately needed the neurological ability to filter it out. That ability comes from a little bit of stimulation to the brain's management center. Every single one of your friends who says they are constantly overstimulated is proving this point perfectly. Their brain's filter is permanently under-performing, leading to a permanent state of internal overwhelm. The coffee provides a brief moment of filter-assisted peace, which feels like calm.
